,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,785,81963,315,2326,12822,315,264,23739,525,8433,11,438,6839,3685,13,220,7379,57960,4044,71089,4779,304,12348,271,58,6405,921,25643,551,7,16,13,17,9961,626,12670,362,11,425,11,356,11,422,11,350,11,1599,11,809,11,1863,280,12670,856,11,379,11,1147,401,87,284,320,17,4999,15,13,17,317,88,284,320,16,13,17,11,15,13,23,317,89,284,320,15,11,17,626,55,284,320,15,11,15,317,56,284,856,280,51,284,379,280,32,284,1147,280,57,284,856,488,379,280,33,284,856,488,1147,280,35,284,379,488,1147,280,34,284,856,488,379,488,1147,401,7633,1188,34,488,422,5620,17,313,7,33,488,356,5620,17,313,7,33,488,809,5620,17,11,1151,317,7633,7644,313,56,313,57,313,34,313,35,313,32,313,21621,317,7633,5349,313,32,317,7633,5349,313,34,317,7633,5349,313,56,317,7633,4140,313,55,11991,13571,317,7633,4140,313,35,11991,13571,317,7633,4140,313,57,11991,13571,626,1502,20912,55,73315,320,34,488,422,5620,17,11,451,317,1502,20912,56,73315,320,33,488,356,5620,17,11,5052,317,1502,20912,57,73315,320,33,488,809,5620,17,11,467,317,24157,6405,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe midpoint of three edges of a cube are connected, as shown below.  Find $\\angle XYZ,$ in degrees\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(1.2 cm);\n\npair A, B, C, D, T, X, Y, Z;\npair x, y, z;\n\nx = (2,-0.2);\ny = (1.2,0.8);\nz = (0,2);\n\nX = (0,0);\nY = x;\nT = y;\nA = z;\nZ = x + y;\nB = x + z;\nD = y + z;\nC = x + y + z;\n\ndraw((C + D)/2--(B + C)/2--(B + Y)/2,red);\ndraw(X--Y--Z--C--D--A--cycle);\ndraw(B--A);\ndraw(B--C);\ndraw(B--Y);\ndraw(T--X,dashed);\ndraw(T--D,dashed);\ndraw(T--Z,dashed);\n\nlabel(\"$X$\", (C + D)/2, N);\nlabel(\"$Y$\", (B + C)/2, SE);\nlabel(\"$Z$\", (B + Y)/2, W);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"c069ae159563ca90e2563d7163ba13556de8e1580801868ba8e2e03f1b263a42"},"seconds":77.91814661026001,"seed":568498800223865000,"stop":"eos","text":"The problem involves finding the measure of the angle $\\angle XYZ$ formed by connecting the midpoints of three edges of a cube. To solve this, we first need to understand the geometric relationship between the points $X$, $Y$, and $Z$ in the context of the cube.\n\nLet's consider a cube with side length $s$. We'll place the cube in a coordinate system where one vertex of the cube is at the origin $(0, 0, 0)$, and the opposite vertex is at $(s, s, s)$. The vertices of the cube are then $(0,0,0)$, $(s,0,0)$, $(0,s,0)$, $(0,0,s)$, $(s,s,0)$, $(s,0,s)$, $(0,s,s)$, and $(s,s,s)$.\n\nThe midpoints of the edges of the cube are points that divide each edge into two equal parts. For simplicity, we can take the midpoints of the edges starting from the origin:\n\n1. The midpoint of the edge from $(0,0,0)$ to $(s,0,0)$ is $\\left(\\frac{s}{2}, 0, 0\\right)$.\n2. The midpoint of the edge from $(0,0,0)$ to $(0,s,0)$ is $\\left(0, \\frac{s}{2}, 0\\right)$.\n3. The midpoint of the edge from $(0,0,0)$ to $(0,0,s)$ is $\\left(0, 0, \\frac{s}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWithout loss of generality, we can assume that points $X$, $Y$, and $Z$ are the midpoints of the edges from $(s, s, 0)$ to $(s, s, s)$, from $(s, s, 0)$ to $(s, 0, 0)$, and from $(s, s, 0)$ to $(0, s, 0)$ respectively. This gives us the coordinates:\n\n- $X = \\left(s, s, \\frac{s}{2}\\right)$\n- $Y = \\left(s, \\frac{s}{2}, 0\\right)$\n- $Z = \\left(\\frac{s}{2}, s, 0\\right)$\n\nTo find the angle $\\angle XYZ$, we need to calculate the vectors $\\overrightarrow{XY}$ and $\\overrightarrow{XZ}$ and use the dot product formula.\n\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{XY}$ is:\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XY} = Y - X = \\left(s, \\frac{s}{2}, 0\\right) - \\left(s, s, \\frac{s}{2}\\right) = \\left(0, -\\frac{s}{2}, -\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\n\\]\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{XZ}$ is:\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XZ} = Z - X = \\left(\\frac{s}{2}, s, 0\\right) - \\left(s, s, \\frac{s}{2}\\right) = \\left(-\\frac{s}{2}, 0, -\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThe dot product $\\overrightarrow{XY} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{XZ}$ is:\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XY} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{XZ} = \\left(0, -\\frac{s}{2}, -\\frac{s}{2}\\right) \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{s}{2}, 0, -\\frac{s}{2}\\right) = 0 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{s}{2}\\right) + \\left(-\\frac{s}{2}\\right) \\cdot 0 + \\left(-\\frac{s}{2}\\right) \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{s}{2}\\right) = \\frac{s^2}{4}\n\\]\n\nThe magnitudes of $\\overrightarrow{XY}$ and $\\overrightarrow{XZ}$ are:\n\\[\n|\\overrightarrow{XY}| = \\sqrt{0^2 + \\left(-\\frac{s}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\frac{s}{2}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{s^2}{4} + \\frac{s^2}{4}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{s^2}{2}} = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{s\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\overrightarrow{XZ}| = \\sqrt{\\left(-\\frac{s}{2}\\right)^2 + 0^2 + \\left(-\\frac{s}{2}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{s^2}{4} + \\frac{s^2}{4}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{s^2}{2}} = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{s\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\nUsing the dot product formula:\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{XY} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{XZ} = |\\overrightarrow{XY}| |\\overrightarrow{XZ}| \\cos \\theta \\implies \\frac{s^2}{4} = \\left(\\frac{s\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{s\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\cos \\theta \\implies \\frac{s^2}{4} = \\frac{s^2}{2} \\cos \\theta \\implies \\cos \\theta = \\frac{1}{2} \\implies \\theta = 60^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of the angle $\\angle XYZ$ is \\(\\boxed{60}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{60}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
